  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Front remains stalled offshore to the S of the\nFA. Convection overnight has been blossoming along the boundary,\nand this will likely continue through the day today. Surface \nflow becomes more onshore latter half of the day with the\nseabreeze, kicking off a period of increasing moisture and \ninstability tomorrow onwards across the Southeast U.S. coastline\nwith a modest uptick of shower and thunderstorm coverage as a \nwave of low pressure meandering over GA along the boundary\nnudges the front toward the coast after sunset tonight.\nExpecting a dry day for most of the area though a generous\n15-20% PoP has been kept from previous forecast iterations in\ntypical seabreeze convergence zones over the Albemarle\nPeninsula and Crystal Coast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The low pressure currently over SECONUS is \nforecast to lift along the stalled front in the early morning\nhours THU, pushing the boundary north toward, and perhaps over,\nthe ENC coast, providing a better focal point for more \nwidespread coverage of showers and storms ahead of a second \nfront which will cross the area by Sat. Given the multiple days \nof onshore flow, PWATs do return back closer to 2\" tomorrow \nonwards. With enough instability in place, and subtly increasing\namounts of shear as trough aloft over CAN digs toward the Great\nLakes, a few isolated stronger storms aren't completely out of \nthe question for FRI. In addition to this, any shower or \nthunderstorm that develops will be an efficient rainmaker, \npotentially bringing torrential rainfall and a low end risk for \nflash flooding, mainly in urban and poor drainage areas. As \nsuch, SPC has added a Marginal Risk (lvl 1/5) of severe tstorms\nand WPC has upgraded to a Slight Risk (lvl 2/5) of excessive \nrainfall for FRI while the FA is warm sectored with the front \nthat has been offshore then being stalled along or near the \nAppMts. Rain continues into the weekend when a front pushed by\nthe digging trough from the Great Lakes will cross the FA on \nSAT. Temps through the weekend remain seasonable with highs in \nthe 80s and lows in the mid 60s to mid 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...SFC high pressure builds back in behind SAT's \nfront on SUN with ridging aloft in place over SECONUS early next\nweek. This will bringing a return to more climatological precip\nchances along the seabreeze as well as rising temperatures, low \n90s/mid 70s splits and MaxAppTs in excess of 100deg from TUES\nonward next week.",
